<div class="essay-body">
<h2 id="s14-day">Why 25 December</h2>
<p>The gospels give the birth of Jesus no date. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nativity-of-jesus">Nativity</a> is told in Matthew and Luke, which agree that Jesus was born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bethlehem">Bethlehem</a> in Judea in the reign of King Herod; reckoning from the death of Herod would place the birth between 6 and 4 BCE, but neither account names a day. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christmas">Christmas</a>, which with Easter marks the year of <a href="/religion/christianity">Christianity</a>, had to find its day later.</p>
<p>The earliest document to place the birth on 25 December is the Chronograph of 354, a Roman calendar, in a part generally agreed to have been written in Rome in 336. The Institute's page for <a href="/today-in-history/december-25">25 December</a> therefore lists the year 336 as the earliest recorded Christmas on that day, and adds that why the day was chosen remains debated.</p>
<p>Part of that debate concerns the sun. In the <a href="/empire/roman-empire">Roman Empire</a> 25 December was the traditional date of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/winter-solstice">winter solstice</a>. On 25 December 274 the emperor Aurelian dedicated a temple in Rome to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sol-invictus">Sol Invictus</a>, the "Unconquered Sun", fulfilling a vow made after his campaign against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palmyra">Palmyra</a> and paying for it with the city's spoils. He promoted Sol Invictus as the chief god of the empire, and according to some scholars he also instituted on that day the festival called Dies Natalis Solis Invicti, the birthday of the unconquered sun. The god appeared on the coinage of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantine-i">Constantine I</a> until 325/326, and the last known inscription that refers to him dates to 387, so the Christmas of 336 was kept while the Sun was still honoured in the empire.</p>
<p>Other midwinter feasts stood near the day. The Roman <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saturnalia">Saturnalia</a>, held on 17 December and extended by the first century BCE to 23 December, brought a public banquet, gift-giving, gambling and a licence in which masters served their slaves at table; it remained popular into the third and fourth centuries CE and may have influenced later midwinter customs of western Europe. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yule">Yule</a>, the winter festival of the heathen Germanic peoples, merged with Christmas during Christianisation; according to Hákonar saga góða, Hákon the Good passed a law in Norway that set Yule at the time when Christians celebrated Christmas. Among the Slavs <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/koliada">Koliada</a> names the period from Christmas to Epiphany and rituals some of which date to pre-Christian times, and the profile of <a href="/religion/slavic-paganism">Slavic paganism</a> names Koliada at Christmastide first among the rites that followed the farming year. The customs of Christmas, its entry says, mix pre-Christian, Christian and secular themes.</p>
<p>Nor is the day one day everywhere. Part of the Eastern Christian churches keep Christmas on 25 December of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/julian-calendar">Julian calendar</a>, which currently corresponds to 7 January, and the Armenian Christmas falls on 6 January, the traditional day of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/epiphany-holiday">Epiphany</a>. Around the day the church built a season. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/advent">Advent</a>, the time of preparation, existed from about 480. In 567 the Council of Tours proclaimed the twelve days from Christmas to Epiphany a sacred and festive season, ending on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/twelfth-night-holiday">Twelfth Night</a>, which traditions place on 5 or 6 January according to whether the counting begins on Christmas Day or on 26 December. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/candlemas">Candlemas</a>, on 2 February, is traditionally the fortieth day and the conclusion of the season.</p>

<h2 id="s14-crowns">A Day for Crowns</h2>
<p>The best-known act of any Christmas Day in the Institute's pages is a coronation. During the Christmas Mass of 800, in the old basilica on the site of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/st-peters-basilica">St. Peter's</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-leo-iii">Pope Leo III</a> crowned the Frankish king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charlemagne">Charlemagne</a>, who was acclaimed Imperator Romanorum, Emperor of the Romans. He was the first emperor in the West since the deposition of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romulus-augustulus">Romulus Augustulus</a> in 476. Leo had himself been elected pope on <a href="/today-in-history/december-26">26 December</a> 795.</p>
<p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coronation-of-charlemagne">coronation of Charlemagne</a> marked the high point of the <a href="/empire/carolingian-empire">Carolingian Empire</a>, whose profile reads it as establishing the ideal of a unified Christian Europe under Carolingian leadership. It also became a model. The <a href="/empire/holy-roman-empire">Holy Roman Empire</a> traced its origin to that Christmas Day, an act that, in its profile's words, deliberately invoked the legacy of the western Roman Empire to legitimise Frankish dominance over Latin Christendom, although most historians treat the coronation of Otto I in 962 as the empire's functional founding. Both profiles list the Christmas coronation among their key events.</p>
<p>Later rulers took the same day. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stephen-i-of-hungary">Stephen I</a>, son of Grand Prince <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/geza-grand-prince-of-the-hungarians">Géza</a>, was crowned at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/esztergom">Esztergom</a> as the first king of Hungary. The date is not certain: the profile of the <a href="/empire/kingdom-of-hungary-1000-1301">Kingdom of Hungary</a> gives Christmas 1000 or 1 January 1001, and the day page says the same. According to Stephen's later legends his crown was sent by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-sylvester-ii">Pope Sylvester II</a> with the approval of Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/otto-iii-holy-roman-emperor">Otto III</a>; the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-crown-of-hungary">Holy Crown of Hungary</a> that survives is traditionally linked with him, though its present form combines Byzantine enamel work of the later eleventh century with a Latin upper part. With the coronation Hungary became a Christian kingdom.</p>
<p>On Christmas Day 1025 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mieszko-ii-lambert">Mieszko II Lambert</a> was crowned king of Poland in the cathedral of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gniezno">Gniezno</a>, where early kings of Poland were crowned. His father, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boleslaw-i-the-brave">Bolesław I the Brave</a>, had become Poland's first crowned king in that same year, the year of his death. The crown did not stay: Mieszko's reign saw attacks by the Holy Roman Empire and Kievan Rus, revolts by his brothers and the loss of the royal crown itself.</p>
<p>On Christmas Day 1046 an emperor was crowned by a pope of his own making. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-iii-holy-roman-emperor">Henry III</a> had been king of Germany since 1039. In 1046 a synod at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sutri">Sutri</a>, held in his presence, removed three rival claimants to the papacy. Henry had Suidger, bishop of Bamberg, made pope as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-clement-ii">Clement II</a>, and Clement crowned him emperor.</p>
<p>None of these pages says why Christmas was chosen. What they show is the setting. Charlemagne was crowned during the Mass of the feast, before the congregation that the feast had gathered, and at Westminster in 1066, as the next section tells, a congregation's acclamation was loud enough to alarm the soldiers outside. A coronation needed a church, a liturgy and witnesses, and the great feast supplied all three; and after 800 the day may also have carried the memory of Charlemagne. That is the Institute's reading of its pages, not a statement any of them makes. The same gathering could serve other ends. On Christmas Day 820 followers of Michael II murdered the Byzantine emperor Leo V the Armenian in a church of the Great Palace of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a>, and Michael took the throne as the first ruler of the Amorian dynasty.</p>

<h2 id="s14-normans">Norman and Crusader Christmases</h2>
<p>In the eleventh and twelfth centuries the Christmas crown passed to conquerors. Duke William of Normandy defeated and killed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harold-godwinson">Harold Godwinson</a> at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-hastings">Battle of Hastings</a> on 14 October 1066, and on Christmas Day he was crowned king of England in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/westminster-abbey">Westminster Abbey</a>, the church rebuilt by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edward-the-confessor">Edward the Confessor</a> and consecrated only the year before. The day did not pass quietly. Norman soldiers, mistaking the congregation's acclamation for a riot, set fire to nearby houses. Every English and British monarch since <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-the-conqueror">William the Conqueror</a> has been crowned in the abbey, except Edward V and Edward VIII, who were never crowned. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norman-conquest">Norman Conquest</a> that the coronation sealed replaced the English elite with a Norman aristocracy and church hierarchy, as the profiles of the <a href="/empire/duchy-of-normandy">Duchy of Normandy</a> and the <a href="/empire/kingdom-of-england">Kingdom of England</a> record.</p>
<p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-crusade">First Crusade</a> carried the custom to the place of the Nativity itself. After the crusaders stormed Jerusalem in July 1099, their leaders chose <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/godfrey-of-bouillon">Godfrey of Bouillon</a> to govern the city; he declined the title of king and was known as Advocate of the Holy Sepulchre. When he died in July 1100 his brother Baldwin, count of Edessa, came south, and on Christmas Day 1100 he was crowned King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baldwin-i-of-jerusalem">Baldwin I</a> at Bethlehem, which Christian tradition holds to be the birthplace of Jesus. The <a href="/empire/kingdom-of-jerusalem">Kingdom of Jerusalem</a> thus received its first crowned king on the feast of the birth, in the town of the birth.</p>
<p>A generation later the Normans of the south did the same. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roger-ii-of-sicily">Roger II</a>, count of Sicily from 1105, had united the Norman lands of southern Italy. During the papal schism of 1130 he supported <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antipope-anacletus-ii">Antipope Anacletus II</a>, who granted him a royal title in return, and on Christmas Day 1130 Roger was crowned in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palermo-cathedral">Palermo Cathedral</a>, in the building that stood before the present one. The crown had to be defended by force: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-innocent-ii">Pope Innocent II</a>, the emperor and rebellious barons and cities fought Roger for most of the 1130s, and only in 1139, after Innocent had been captured, did the pope recognise him as king. The profile of the <a href="/empire/kingdom-of-sicily">Kingdom of Sicily</a> begins the kingdom's history with that grant and that Christmas.</p>
<p>The <a href="/empire/armenian-kingdom-of-cilicia">Armenian Kingdom of Cilicia</a> shows the same choice in another calendar. For its first century Cilicia was a principality whose rulers Byzantium would not recognise as kings. Prince Levon II, with the support of the emperors <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-barbarossa">Frederick Barbarossa</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-vi-holy-roman-emperor">Henry VI</a>, was crowned in the cathedral of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tarsus">Tarsus</a> on 6 January 1198, the Armenian Christmas, by the Catholicos Gregory VI Abirad.</p>
<p>The feast was no protection. On <a href="/today-in-history/december-24">Christmas Eve</a> 1144, after a siege begun on 28 November, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imad-al-din-zengi">Imad al-Din Zengi</a>, atabeg of Mosul and Aleppo and founder of the <a href="/empire/zengid-dynasty">Zengid dynasty</a>, took Edessa, the capital of the first of the crusader states, which Baldwin had founded in 1098. Its fall prompted the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-crusade">Second Crusade</a>.</p>
<p>Smaller courts show why the season suited acts of state. In Norway the law code of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hird">hird</a>, the royal household, was read when the whole hird assembled at Christmas and Easter. In Connacht the king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uilliam-buidhe-o-ceallaigh">Uilliam Buidhe Ó Ceallaigh</a> held a great feast at Christmas 1351 for the poets, historians and musicians of Ireland, which a poem celebrated. And at Roskilde, at Christmas 1026, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cnut-the-great">Cnut the Great</a> had his regent and brother-in-law <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ulf-jarl">Ulf Jarl</a> killed after a quarrel over chess. At Christmas a king's men were all in one place.</p>

<h2 id="s14-surprise">A Day for Surprise</h2>
<p>Voyages do not stop for feasts, and two of them met land at Christmas. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/santa-maria-ship">Santa María</a>, the flagship of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christopher-columbus">Christopher Columbus</a>'s first voyage, ran aground off the north coast of Hispaniola on Christmas Day 1492. From its timbers Columbus built the fort of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/la-navidad">La Navidad</a>, the first European settlement in the Americas, in the territory of the Taíno chief <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guacanagari">Guacanagarí</a>, and left some 39 men there. When he returned in late 1493 the fort had been destroyed and its men killed, an attack attributed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caonabo">Caonabo</a> of Maguana. In the Pacific, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/james-cook">James Cook</a> sighted the atoll of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kiritimati">Kiritimati</a> on Christmas Eve 1777; it is also known as Christmas Island.</p>
<p>Armies have used the day as well. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khmelnytsky-uprising">Khmelnytsky Uprising</a> against the Polish–Lithuanian Commonwealth, after his victories over the Poles, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bohdan-khmelnytsky">Bohdan Khmelnytsky</a> entered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyiv">Kyiv</a> at Christmas 1648, hailed as a liberator from Polish captivity. The profile of the <a href="/empire/cossack-hetmanate">Cossack Hetmanate</a> opens its history of the state with that entry.</p>
<p>The best-known surprise came in 1776. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/american-revolutionary-war">American Revolutionary War</a> was going badly for the Continental Army, which had been defeated in New York and had retreated through New Jersey. On the night of 25–26 December <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/george-washington">George Washington</a>, its commander-in-chief, led a column from Pennsylvania across the icy Delaware River at McConkey's Ferry, in weather that turned from rain to sleet and snow. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/george-washingtons-crossing-of-the-delaware-river">The crossing</a> was meant to be one of several; the other crossings planned in support were called off or ineffective, leaving about 2,400 men for the attack.</p>
<p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-trenton">Battle of Trenton</a> was fought on the morning of 26 December. The garrison of about 1,400 Hessian auxiliaries under Colonel Johann Rall had no long-distance outposts or patrols and was caught off guard. The Hessians lost 22 killed, among them Rall, and 896 captured; the Americans suffered two deaths on the march and five wounded, and crossed back to Pennsylvania with their prisoners and captured stores. The victory raised the army's waning morale and inspired re-enlistments. The entries do not say that the date was chosen because it was Christmas, nor that the feast explains the missing patrols; they record a night crossing in a storm and an enemy that was not watching.</p>
<p>In one case the pages do tie a rising to the holiday. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baptist-war">Baptist War</a>, also called the Christmas Rebellion, began on 25 December 1831 in western Jamaica. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samuel-sharpe">Samuel Sharpe</a>, an enslaved Baptist deacon, intended a peaceful general strike for wages; it turned into an armed revolt of up to 60,000 enslaved people in which many plantations were burned. It was crushed by early 1832, hundreds were executed, and Sharpe was hanged in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/montego-bay">Montego Bay</a> in May. The profile of <a href="/movement/abolitionism">Abolitionism</a> records that the brutal suppression, reported by missionaries, helped persuade a newly reformed Parliament to pass the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery-abolition-act-1833">Slavery Abolition Act</a> of 1833.</p>
<p>The twentieth century added its own. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christmas-battles">Christmas Battles</a> were a Russian offensive against German positions near Riga in late December 1916 and January 1917, in which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latvian-riflemen">Latvian Riflemen</a> broke through the German lines in heavy winter fighting, at a cost of several thousand casualties; the profile of the <a href="/empire/republic-of-latvia">Republic of Latvia</a> counts the battles part of national memory. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christmas-uprising">Christmas Uprising</a> in Montenegro, a revolt against union with Serbia, took place in early January 1919, around Orthodox Christmas. And <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bloody-christmas-1920">Bloody Christmas</a> names the Italian attack on Fiume from 24 to 29 December 1920, which ended the occupation of the city by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gabriele-dannunzio">Gabriele D'Annunzio</a>.</p>

<h2 id="s14-truce">A Day for Truce</h2>
<p>If Christmas has been a day for attack, it has also been a day for stopping. On Christmas Eve 1814 British and American negotiators at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghent">Ghent</a> signed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-ghent">Treaty of Ghent</a>, which ended the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-of-1812">War of 1812</a> and restored the pre-war borders of June 1812. News of the treaty took a month to reach the United States, and in the meantime American forces under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andrew-jackson">Andrew Jackson</a> won the Battle of New Orleans on 8 January 1815. The treaty took effect in February 1815, when ratified copies were exchanged in Washington.</p>
<p>A century later the stopping came from below. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christmas-truce">Christmas truce</a> of 1914 was a series of widespread unofficial ceasefires, mainly along the Western Front of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a>, involving roughly 100,000 British and German troops. On Christmas Eve soldiers along parts of the front sang carols across the lines. In some areas men from both sides went into no man's land on Christmas Eve and Christmas Day to exchange food and souvenirs; there were joint burial ceremonies, carols and games of football. Hostilities continued in other sectors.</p>
<p>The truce was not what governments had wanted. On 7 December 1914 Pope Benedict XV had begged the warring governments for an official truce, and both sides refused. What happened instead was arranged by the men in the trenches, and it was not allowed to become a custom: the following year only a few units arranged ceasefires, in part because of orders from commanders prohibiting truces.</p>
<p>The idea that a carol could stop a war is older than 1914. A legend attached to <a href="/songs/o-holy-night">O Holy Night</a>, the French "Cantique de Noël" of 1847, holds that French troops sang it on Christmas Eve 1870, in the Franco-Prussian War, and brought about a brief ceasefire. The song's page gives it as a legend, and so does this essay.</p>

<h2 id="s14-forbidden">Forbidden and Reinvented</h2>
<p>The feast itself has been contested. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/puritans">Puritans</a>, English Protestants who sought to purify the Church of England of practices they considered Catholic, were prominent on the parliamentary side in the Civil War, and they prohibited the celebration of Christmas in England from 1647 to 1660. The entry on Christmas says no more than that. The prohibition ended in the year of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stuart-restoration">Stuart Restoration</a>, and one carol crosses it: an early version of <a href="/songs/god-rest-ye-merry-gentlemen">God Rest Ye Merry, Gentlemen</a> is in an anonymous manuscript of the 1650s. By 1663 the diary of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samuel-pepys">Samuel Pepys</a> mentions the "Christmas boxes" of money or presents that tradesmen collected after Christmas, a custom that may lie behind the name of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boxing-day">Boxing Day</a>, though the origin of the name is not definitive.</p>
<p>The songs were by then old. The first known Christmas hymns may be traced to fourth-century Rome, the century of the Chronograph. By the thirteenth century popular Christmas songs in the vernacular had developed in France, Germany and particularly Italy, under the influence of Francis of Assisi, to whom the first <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nativity-scene">nativity scene</a> is also attributed: he is said to have staged it at Christmas 1223 in a cave near Greccio. The words of <a href="/songs/in-dulci-jubilo">In dulci jubilo</a>, mixing medieval German and Latin, are thought to date from around 1328. The earliest English <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christmas-carol">carols</a> appear in a manuscript of 1426 by the Shropshire chaplain John Awdlay, who recorded twenty-five. Carols grew in popularity after the Reformation, when reformers such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/martin-luther">Martin Luther</a> promoted congregational singing, and they travelled with missions: the <a href="/songs/huron-carol">Huron Carol</a>, counted as Canada's oldest Christmas song, was written, probably in 1642, by the Jesuit <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jean-de-brebeuf">Jean de Brébeuf</a> in the language of the Huron-Wendat people.</p>
<p>The eighteenth and nineteenth centuries rebuilt the feast in song, print and custom. Charles Wesley wrote <a href="/songs/hark-the-herald-angels-sing">Hark! The Herald Angels Sing</a> in 1739 and called it "Hymn for Christmas-Day"; other hands revised its words, and its present tune, from a work by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/felix-mendelssohn">Felix Mendelssohn</a>, was fitted only in 1855. <a href="/songs/silent-night">Silent Night</a> was first sung on Christmas Eve 1818 in the parish church of Oberndorf, to a guitar, the priest Joseph Mohr having brought his words that day to the organist Franz Xaver Gruber; flooding of the river had possibly damaged the church organ, and the story that the organ had broken down comes from a fictional tale of the 1930s. The carol has been translated into about 300 languages.</p>
<p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christmas-tree">Christmas tree</a> came from Central Europe. The custom was first recorded among German Lutherans in the sixteenth century, and records indicate that a tree was placed in the Cathedral of Strasbourg in 1539. In Britain the German-born Queen <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charlotte-of-mecklenburg-strelitz">Charlotte</a> introduced a tree in 1800, and the custom spread more widely after the marriage of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/queen-victoria">Queen Victoria</a> to Prince Albert. <a href="/songs/o-tannenbaum">O Tannenbaum</a>, published in 1824, was built on an older folk song that had nothing to do with the holiday, and it is not, strictly, about such a tree. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/santa-claus">Santa Claus</a> derives from the folklore of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-nicholas">Saint Nicholas</a>, the fourth-century bishop of Myra, and from the Dutch Sinterklaas, whose legend settlers brought to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-amsterdam">New Amsterdam</a>; the profile of <a href="/empire/new-netherland">New Netherland</a> counts him among the colony's legacies.</p>
<p>The season's best-known book appeared in the same decades. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/a-christmas-carol">A Christmas Carol</a>, by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-dickens">Charles Dickens</a>, was published in London on 19 December 1843. Dickens had been inspired by a visit to the Field Lane Ragged School for London's street children, and his miser Scrooge, visited by the spirits of Christmas Past, Present and Yet to Come, becomes a kinder man. The first edition sold out by Christmas Eve, and thirteen editions had appeared by the end of 1844; Dickens gave public readings of it from 1849 until 1870, the year of his death. In 1853 <a href="/songs/good-king-wenceslas">Good King Wenceslas</a> set a similar lesson on the day after Christmas: its tenth-century ruler of Bohemia, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wenceslaus-i-duke-of-bohemia">Wenceslaus I</a>, in his lifetime only a duke, goes out in harsh weather to give alms to a poor peasant on the Feast of Stephen, 26 December.</p>
<p>Not all the season's music was made for it. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/messiah-handel">Messiah</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/george-frideric-handel">George Frideric Handel</a>, composed in 1741, received its first performance in Dublin on 13 April 1742, at a charity concert, in spring and not at Christmas; Handel's opera Agrippina, however, had its premiere in Venice on 26 December 1709. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/the-nutcracker">The Nutcracker</a>, the ballet by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pyotr-ilyich-tchaikovsky">Tchaikovsky</a> whose plot is set on Christmas Eve, was first performed on 18 December 1892 in Saint Petersburg and was not deemed a success; only since the late 1960s has it been danced by many companies, especially in North America.</p>

<h2 id="s14-century">The Twentieth Century</h2>
<p>In the twentieth century the day belonged to broadcasting. Bing Crosby gave the first public performance of <a href="/songs/white-christmas">White Christmas</a> on his radio show on Christmas Day 1941, a few weeks after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/attack-on-pearl-harbor">attack on Pearl Harbor</a>. Its mixture of melancholy and comforting images of home has often been said to have spoken especially strongly to listeners during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a>, and the Armed Forces Network was flooded with requests for it. Writers on popular music see the song, written by a Jewish immigrant to the United States, as the proof that secular Christmas songs could succeed commercially.</p>
<p>On Christmas Eve 1968 the audience was larger still. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/apollo-8">Apollo 8</a>, flown from 21 to 27 December, was the first human spaceflight to reach the Moon. Frank Borman, Jim Lovell and William Anders orbited it ten times without landing and were the first humans to see and photograph its far side and an Earthrise. On Christmas Eve they read the first ten verses of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/book-of-genesis">Book of Genesis</a> in a television broadcast seen by millions.</p>
<p>The century's last great Christmas act was an ending. The <a href="/empire/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> had been coming apart through 1991: a coup by hardliners failed in August, the republics declared independence one after another, and on 8 December the leaders of Russia, Ukraine and Belarus agreed to replace the union with a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/commonwealth-of-independent-states">Commonwealth of Independent States</a>. On 25 December <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mikhail-gorbachev">Mikhail Gorbachev</a> resigned as Soviet president in a televised address. That evening the Soviet flag was lowered from the Kremlin and replaced by the Russian tricolour, and the next day the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dissolution-of-the-soviet-union">Soviet Union formally ceased to exist</a>.</p>
<p>The Soviet state's own winter festival was the New Year. Its gift-bringer was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ded-moroz">Ded Moroz</a>, Father Frost: declared in 1928 an ally of the priest and the kulak, he was revived after a letter published in Pravda in 1935 and became the main symbol of the Soviet New Year's holiday, bringing his presents at New Year. The pages do not say why Gorbachev chose 25 December, and nothing in them suggests that the feast had any part in it. Here the pattern of the crowns runs out: the feast on which an emperor was made in 800 is, in the pages on 1991, a date without further meaning.</p>

<h2 id="s14-data">What the Data Shows</h2>
<p>When this essay was written, in November 2026, the Institute's day pages held 3679 events, 300 of them in <a href="/today-in-history/december">December</a>; the months run from 279 events in November to 364 in January. Across the year a day has between 8 and 44 events, with a median of 10, and 24, 25 and 26 December each have 10. Christmas Day is thus an ordinary day by count. It is not ordinary by age. The events of 24 December run from 502 to 1968 and those of 26 December from 795 to 2004, but those of 25 December run from 274 to 1991 and lie early: 2 fall before 500, 6 between 500 and 1499, 1 between 1500 and 1799 and 1 from 1800 on. The table lists them. The day pages also list the people of the encyclopedia who were born or died on each day: 10 born and 3 died on 24 December, 5 born and 8 died on 25 December, 6 born and 7 died on 26 December.</p>
