<p>The encyclopedia tells the same story from another side. The texts of 64 entries name Christmas: 30 works, 12 ideas, 11 events, 6 people, 4 places and 1 group, and no polity, institution or term. The works are mostly carols, the ideas the customs and neighbouring feasts of the season. Of the 64, 18 name Christmas Day or Christmas Eve, among them the entries for the coronation of Charlemagne, the Santa María, the Christmas truce and Apollo 8. In the profiles' texts the word Christmas occurs 10 times, and in their key events and takeaways 2 times, both for the coronation of 800.</p>
<p>These figures describe the Institute's pages, not the past. The day pages are a selection, made event by event from what is firmly dated to a day, and a medieval coronation on a great feast is exactly the kind of event whose day a chronicler wrote down; the early weight of 25 December reflects that as much as any habit of rulers. Nor are the three days a Christian calendar only. On 24 December the pages record Emperor Wu of Liang making his infant son crown prince in 502, a Fatimid victory over the Qarmatians at the gates of Cairo in 971 and a Maratha victory at Bhopal in 1737, events for which the date carried no such meaning. And the day after Christmas 2004 is given to an earthquake and tsunami that killed about 230,000 people in 14 countries around the Indian Ocean, which no reading of the calendar explains.</p>
